{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And he called.</b> By reason's way, Moses and Aaron were not on the night of the fifteenth in the land of Rameses with Israel, but in Egypt, which was the seat of kingship, where Pharaoh was. Now they went out from the doorway of their house by HaShem's command at midnight, and Pharaoh gave them permission that Israel should go to serve HaShem. Now Moses went with Pharaoh's servants to Rameses to bring Israel out. And the day is known, for between Old Egypt, where Joseph's storehouses are known to this day, and Rameses is six parasangs. Now they began to go out in the morning, which is the time of the dawn column rising, when the sun's light begins to appear in the clouds. And between the beginning of this moment and sunrise is one full hour and a third of an hour. Now we have: \"בקר\" (morning) and the darkness is great, as in: \"and she lay at his feet until בקר\" (Ruth 3:14), and there it is written: \"before one man could recognize his fellow.\" Now there were those of Israel who went out at the beginning of the dawn column, while it was still night by the Torah's reckoning, until sunrise, and these were near to Egypt. And the others, who were far, went out that same day, for it was a great multitude, and there is more from the beginning of Rameses to its end than eight parasangs. Therefore it is written: \"YHWH your Elohim brought you out of Egypt לילה (by night)\" (Deuteronomy 16:1), and afterward it is written: \"היום אתם יוצאים (today you are going out)\" (below, 13:4), and further: \"and the children of Israel went out ביד רמה (with a high hand)\" (Numbers 33:3), and it is written there: \"and the Egyptians were burying\" (Numbers 33:4), and they buried by day. And I have already explained:" "גם אתם. כדברכם. That you go a three days' journey, just as you spoke, and also: וגם מקננו ילך עמנו (and also our livestock will go with us) (below, 10:26):"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The argument is compressed and elliptical throughout; the colon at the end is literal, indicating a trailing reference to a prior explanation.", 1 "The trailing colon reproduces the Hebrew text as printed, which ends with the citation formula rather than an explanatory sentence."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:57.380120873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}